<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Dikwa Emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1901 CE – 1961 CE · capital Dikwa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Kanuri emirate south of Lake Chad, carved out of the old Bornu lands that fell to German Kamerun, which joined Nigeria after the 1961 plebiscite.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dikwa">Dikwa</a> had been the capital of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rabih-az-zubayrs-state">Rabih az-Zubayr</a>, the Sudanese warlord who conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> in 1893. After the French killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabih-az-zubayr">Rabih</a> in 1900, the European powers restored the al-Kanemi dynasty, but the old lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu Empire</a> were split by the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kamerun">Kamerun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>. The part around Dikwa fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where a separate shehu was installed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> kept the Kanuri institutions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a>: a shehu with titled officials, district heads and Islamic courts. Under the Germans, and after 1916 under British rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-cameroons">Northern Cameroons</a>, it was administered through the shehu, with little development. Most people were Kanuri, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuwa-arabs">Shuwa Arab</a> and other farmers and herders.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 1961 plebiscite the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-cameroons">Northern Cameroons</a> voted to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dikwa">Dikwa</a> became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-region-nigeria">Nigeria's Northern Region</a>, joining its Kanuri kin in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/borno-emirate">Borno Emirate</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> survives as a traditional institution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borno-state">Borno State</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1901 CE – 1961 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:97.53%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1901 CE – 1961 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dikwa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kanuri, Shuwa Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Emirate of Dikwa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1931&amp;focus=dikwa-emirate" aria-label="Dikwa Emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5062 1729 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dikwa Emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5382 1985L5399 1946M5382 1985L5256 2278M5382 1985L5256 2278"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5399 1946h0M5256 2278h0M5256 2278h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5382 1985h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5382 1985h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1931&amp;focus=dikwa-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
